From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] MdeModulePkg/Logo: Omarchy's logo instead of TianoCore's

What: replaces the boot logo of edk2-stable202408 (MdeModulePkg/Logo/Logo.bmp,
the TianoCore logo, 193 x 58, 8-bit) with Omarchy's wordmark: 1215 x 285,
24-bit, Omarchy green (#a8cd76, the colour of Omarchy's Plymouth logo) on
black. LogoDxe scales it down when the screen is too small for it: the
biggest whole number of pixels a cell that fits (the logo is 81 x 19
cells), so the edges stay sharp. Nothing else in edk2 changes.

Why: OmacVM.app boots its VMs through this firmware; the first thing a VM
shows should be Omarchy, not TianoCore. At 1920 x 1080 (a cold start) the
logo is as big as OmacVM.app's start animation draws it. After a reboot the
firmware draws at the window's pixel size, and BootLogoLib skips a logo
bigger than the screen: a window under 1215 pixels wide (a small one on a
non-Retina display) got no logo at all.

How it was made: boot-logo/make-logo-bmp.py from logo.svg of Omarchy
(github.com/basecamp/omarchy, commit 6520ac7d, MIT, (c) David Heinemeier
Hansson, boot-logo/LICENSE.omarchy):
  make-logo-bmp.py logo.svg MdeModulePkg/Logo/Logo.bmp 15
Each cell of the logo's 15-unit grid is a 15 x 15 pixel block. The smaller
logo takes each cell's middle pixel (Logo.c, FitLogo).

Upstream: OmacVM only (branding), not for edk2 or QEMU.

Test: Tests/firmware/test-firmware.py boots the built firmware with an empty
disk and checks that the screen shows exactly this bitmap, centred, at
1920 x 1080, and the same logo at 13 pixels a cell on a 1100 x 700 screen
(build-qemu-gpu-runtime.sh runs it; on failure the build keeps QEMU's
firmware).

Applied with `git apply` (a binary patch; it checks the original logo's hash).

diff --git a/MdeModulePkg/Logo/Logo.bmp b/MdeModulePkg/Logo/Logo.bmp
index 3e85229e17595ba1f9c59e13692a4f8362ebc850..23ee716cb9984ecb939a266a2f1782ea236d42f9 100644
GIT binary patch
